description:
Security weakness in the committed config, independent of whether the repo is published —
though publishing makes it trivially discoverable.

PROBLEM
configuration.nix lines 29-42 define the eduroam NetworkManager profile:

    "802-1x" = {
      eap = "peap;";
      identity = "$EDUROAM_IDENTITY";
      password = "$EDUROAM_PASSWORD";
      password-flags = "0";
      phase2-auth = "mschapv2";
      system-ca-certs = "true";
    };

`system-ca-certs = "true"` validates that the RADIUS server presents a certificate signed by
some CA in the system trust store. It does NOT check WHICH server. Any attacker running an
access point that broadcasts the SSID "eduroam" with a certificate from any public CA will be
trusted by this supplicant, at which point PEAP proceeds to phase 2 and the client sends its
MSCHAPv2 challenge-response. MSCHAPv2 is crackable offline (DES-based, reducible to a single
56-bit key search), so this yields the institutional password. The credential in question is a
Brown University login, which is SSO-adjacent.

Separately, `password-flags = "0"` means "store the secret in the NetworkManager system
connection file", i.e. the password sits in cleartext in /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/
readable by root. That is a lesser issue and is the standard trade-off for headless autoconnect,
but it is worth recording.

FIX
Add server identity pinning to the 802-1x block. Either of:

    domain-suffix-match = "<brown radius domain, e.g. brown.edu>";

or the stricter, certificate-subject form:

    altsubject-matches = "DNS:<exact radius hostname>";

The correct value must come from Brown IT documentation or from inspecting the certificate of a
known-good association (`journalctl -u wpa_supplicant` during a successful connect shows the
subject). Do not guess it: a wrong value breaks the connection outright.

Optionally also pin the CA explicitly with `ca-cert` pointing at the institutional root rather
than trusting the whole system store.

ACCEPTANCE
- The 802-1x block contains a domain-suffix-match or altsubject-matches entry.
- eduroam still associates successfully after `just deploy`.

Wire the erwin-linkding flake into /etc/nixos and enable the drainer for linkding. Depends on lkd and sops in this repo, and CROSS-REPO on the erwin-linkding issue "nixm" (the module) being built, plus "dply" (Netlify live). Do this after those.

Steps (edit /etc/nixos/flake.nix + machines/homework/configuration.nix):
1. Add flake input erwin-linkding.url pointing at the repo (github/tangled/git+ssh as appropriate); run nix flake lock to pin.
2. Import inputs.erwin-linkding.nixosModules.default into the homework config.
3. Configure:
   services.erwin-drainer.linkding = {
     enable = true;
     netlifyUrl = "https://linkding.ohrg.org";
     queue = "linkding";
     targetUrl = "http://127.0.0.1:9090/api/bookmarks/";
     environmentFile = config.sops.secrets."erwin-linkding/drainer-env".path;  # DRAIN_SECRET + TARGET_TOKEN
     interval = "30min";
   };
4. Deploy and confirm the oneshot runs at boot and the timer is scheduled.

Acceptance: `systemctl list-timers | grep erwin-drainer-linkding` shows the 30-min timer; `systemctl start erwin-drainer-linkding` drains the Netlify queue into local linkding; logs clean.
